spec sk11-materialised-conflict-increment: 2 sessions, 20 permutations
# SK11, increment arm (SET g.v = g.v + 1). SK01 write skew, materialised: each transaction also writes the
# shared guard node g in the statement that takes its doctor off call. This is the
# documented snapshot-isolation remedy (Fekete et al., TODS 2005) for the absent
# locking read (G4).
# The two increments collide on g, so the second writer is refused and the rule
# (at least one doctor on call) holds in every interleaving where both would commit.

starting permutation: s1r s1w s1c s2r s2w s2c
step s1r: MATCH (d:Doctor) WHERE d.oncall = true RETURN count(d) AS oncall
oncall
------
2     
(1 row)
step s1w: MATCH (d:Doctor {name:'alice'}) SET d.oncall = false WITH d MATCH (g:Guard {name:'g'}) SET g.v = g.v + 1 RETURN d.name AS who, g.v AS guard
who    |guard
-------+-----
"alice"|1    
(1 row)
step s1c: COMMIT
step s2r: MATCH (d:Doctor) WHERE d.oncall = true RETURN count(d) AS oncall
oncall
------
2     
(1 row)
step s2w: MATCH (d:Doctor {name:'bob'}) SET d.oncall = false WITH d MATCH (g:Guard {name:'g'}) SET g.v = g.v + 1 RETURN d.name AS who, g.v AS guard
ERROR: serialization conflict
step s2c: COMMIT
ERROR: cypher: transaction poisoned by a prior failed Exec statement — call Rollback
final final: MATCH (d:Doctor) WHERE d.oncall = true WITH count(d) AS oncall MATCH (g:Guard {name:'g'}) RETURN oncall, g.v AS guard
oncall|guard
------+-----
1     |1    
(1 row)

